<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=tunnel-of-eupalinos">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tunnel of Eupalinos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Eupalinian aqueduct</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tunnel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eupalinos">Eupalinos</a> is an aqueduct tunnel 1,036 metres long cut through Mount Kastro on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> in the sixth century BCE, under the tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates</a>. Dug from both ends at once under the engineer Eupalinos of Megara, it carried water to the ancient city on the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythagoreio">Pythagoreio</a>. It is the second known tunnel excavated from both ends and the first planned by geometry, and is part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tunnel-of-eupalinos.jpg" alt="Tunnel of Eupalinos" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moumouza at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eupalinian_aqueduct.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The tunnel was dug in the mid-6th century BCE by two groups working under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eupalinos">Eupalinos of Megara</a>, to bring fresh water to the ancient capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>, today called Pythagoreion. The city had outgrown its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> and cisterns, and its main source of fresh water lay on the other side of Mount Kastro. Running underground, the aqueduct could not easily be found and cut by an enemy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> describes it in his Histories (3.60) as the work of a Megarian named Eupalinus, son of Naustrophus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Water came from a spring near the modern village of Ayiades, about 52 metres above sea level, and ran through a buried channel 890 metres long to the northern mouth. The tunnel is generally 1.8 by 1.8 metres, the northern half in places just wide enough for one person. Its floor lies about 3 metres above the water at the source, so a separate channel, 4 to 8.5 metres deep, was dug beneath it, where the water ran in a pipe of some 5,000 terracotta sections.</p><p>Only two diggers could work at a face, so it was cut from both ends at once. H. J. Kienast calculated that they could cut 12 to 15 centimetres of stone a day, so the work took at least eight years. About 273 metres from the northern end, water, weak rock and mud forced a diversion to the west. To make sure the two halves met, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eupalinos">Eupalinos</a> turned both towards each other, and they join at a dog-leg; at the meeting point their altitudes differed by only a few decimetres.</p><p>The aqueduct was used for 1,100 years before it began to silt up. In the 7th century CE the southern section became a refuge, with a cistern built 400 metres from the southern entrance to collect water dripping from the rock.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Prompted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, scholars began searching for the tunnel in the 19th century. Victor Guérin identified the spring in 1853, and in 1882 an attempt to clear the tunnel for reuse was abandoned, though it allowed Ernst Fabricius to investigate it for the German Archaeological Institute. Ulf Jantzen cleared its full length from 1971 to 1973, and Kienast made a detailed survey. It is on the UNESCO World Heritage List with Pythagoreion and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraion-of-samos">Heraion of Samos</a>, and parts are open to the public.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tunnel of Eupalinos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Tunnel of Eupalinos</b>, 1,036 metres long, was dug from both ends of a mountain at once in the sixth century BCE, and the two teams met almost exactly.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Polycrates, Samos gained a harbour mole, the <b>Tunnel of Eupalinos</b>, more than a kilometre long, which carried water through a mountain to the city, and a vast new temple at the Heraion of Samos, the three works that Herodotus counted the greatest in the Greek world.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>mid-6th century BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Samos, Greece</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/eupalinos">Eupalinos</a> · <a href="/empire/samos">Samos</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The date is not entirely clear: Herodotus mentions the tunnel in his account of the tyrant Polycrates, who ruled c. 540 to 522 BCE, but does not say he built it. It is called the second known tunnel excavated from both ends.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">Aqueduct<span>c. 2000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=tunnel-of-eupalinos">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
